  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A drier, warmer, and increasingly sunny Monday.\nLow clouds and rain showers return Tuesday/Tuesday night with \na rumble of thunder possible. \n\nLatest satellite data showed some breaks in the clouds from\nsoutheast NH, across north central MA, into portions of \nnorthern CT. More stubborn lower clouds remained across \nsoutheast MA and much of RI. Tweaked the timing of this clearing\nfor today. This timing will be crucial for temperatures as \nwell. Full sunshine should be able to boost temperatures towards\nthe upper 60s to lower 70s with 850mb temperatures of +10-15C. \nThe longer the clouds linger, the more likely temperatures stay\nin the 60s. Tweaked afternoon temperatures down slightly this \nmorning, but they should still be a least 10 degrees higher \nacross interior southern New England than Sunday.\n\nThings change tonight as a low center over the Great Lakes \napproaches while high pressure sits over Nova Scotia. This \ninitially turns surface flow out of the northeast advecting \ncooler, saturated air onshore making for low clouds with patchy \ndrizzle to start Tuesday. This brings highs back into the 60s. \nThe warm front lifts north Tuesday afternoon bringing scattered \nrain showers which continue until the cold front drops through \nsometime Wednesday. SNE will be socked in with clouds all of \nTuesday and Tuesday night as moisture remains trapped under a \nstrong inversion. Could even see some rumbles of thunder given \nsome weak elevated instability above that inversion. Elevated \ndewpoints will also lead to mild lows, in the upper 50s and \nlower 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying out Wednesday, and especially Thursday,\nunder high pressure.\n\nWednesday things begin to dry out behind the cold front and the \ninversion mixes out allowing for a mix of clouds and sun.\nHowever, a stronger reinforcing cold front brings a much cooler \nand drier airmass for Thursday as high pressure arrives. 925mb\ntemps drop to near 6C leading to highs only in the low 60s under \nmostly sunny skies. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Next, stronger storm system arrives Friday into\nSaturday.\n\nOur next storm, this one likely stronger than the mid week\nsystem, originates out west Thursday. A low forms in the lee of \nthe Rockies, phasing with another low over the Great Lakes as it\nis swept east by a northern stream trough. It then strengthens\nas it moves over or near New England Friday into Saturday. This \npulls a lot of moisture into the region and with good dynamics \nincluding potential for a strong 45-55kt LLJ and favorable\nplacement beneath the RRQ of a 160kt upper jet, we should see a \nwindy and very rainy Friday-Saturday. Timing and other details \nare uncertain at this time, but confidence in at least a\ndecently strong weather system is increasing.",
